<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humberto Castelo Branco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Humberto Castelo Branco was a Brazilian marshal who led the military coup of 1964 against President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-goulart">João Goulart</a> and became the first president of the military regime, serving until 1967. He used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutional-acts">Institutional Acts</a> to purge opponents, abolished the old parties and created a two-party system. He died in an air crash months after leaving office.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humberto-castelo-branco.jpg" alt="Humberto Castelo Branco" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humberto_Castelo_Branco,_presidente_do_Brasil,_1964_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Humberto de Alencar Castelo Branco was born on 20 September 1897 into a wealthy family of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> with roots in Portugal. His father, Cândido Borges Castelo Branco, was a general, and his mother, Antonieta de Alencar, belonged to the family of the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-alencar">José de Alencar</a>. He joined the army at the Rio Pardo Military School, entered the Military School of Realengo in 1918 and was commissioned a second lieutenant in 1921. He took part in the Revolution of 1930 and later studied at the French War College.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he headed the operations section of the Brazilian Expeditionary Force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he planned manoeuvres at the Battle of Monte Castello and gained great prestige, according to Marshal Cordeiro de Farias. As a general he commanded the Command and General Staff College from 1954 to 1956, and he was chief of staff of the army from 13 September 1963 until April 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Castelo Branco was one of the leaders of the 1964 coup that overthrew Goulart. Congress chose him on 11 April to complete Goulart's term, and he took the oath on 15 April 1964. The First Institutional Act gave him emergency powers, including the power to cancel the political rights of subversive elements for ten years, and he intended to hand power to a civilian in 1966, but hard-line officers backed by War Minister Artur da Costa e Silva wished to stay in power. When opposition candidates won the governorships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> and Guanabara in October 1965, he refused to annul the results, and Costa e Silva persuaded the hard-liners to accept them in return for a promise of a tougher policy.</p><p>On 27 October 1965 he issued the Second Institutional Act, which abolished all existing political parties, restored his emergency powers and extended his term to 1967. Two parties replaced them: the pro-government National Renewal Alliance, ARENA, and the opposition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-democratic-movement">Brazilian Democratic Movement</a>, MDB. In 1967 a commission of jurists drafted a new constitution, and a press law, which remained valid until 2009, was issued near the end of his term. His government relied on loans from the World Bank and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> and on American investment. He was succeeded by Costa e Silva at midnight on 15 March 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Four months after leaving office Castelo Branco died on 18 July 1967 in a mid-air collision of two Piper aircraft near Fortaleza. He belonged to a more liberal legalist faction within the regime, as opposed to his more authoritarian successors, and he was the first leader of the military regime that lasted after the coup of 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Humberto de Alencar Castelo Branco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marshal <b>Humberto Castelo Branco</b> became the first of five generals who ruled as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/brazilian-military-dictatorship">Brazilian military dictatorship</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fortaleza, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in Fortaleza, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.73,-38.53&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Fortaleza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3481 2303 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Humberto Castelo Branco"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3931 2573h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3931 2573h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.73,-38.53&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
